Receptionist: Good morning, Northbridge Adult Learning Centre. How can I help?
Customer: Hello. I am organising a Saturday photography workshop for a local community group. We need a room for a whole day. I saw a price list online, but I wanted to check a few things before booking.
Receptionist: Of course. Our smaller rooms are used for language classes on Saturdays, but there are two larger ones available next month. First, could I take your surname?
Customer: It is Hartley. That is H A R T L E Y. My first name is Robin. I am booking for the group, although the council will pay the invoice.
Receptionist: Thank you. And what address should appear on that invoice?
Customer: The community office, number seventeen, Mill Road. It is the old stone building beside the bridge. We moved from Station Road last spring, so please do not use the address you might already have for us.
Receptionist: I have changed that. Which month are you hoping to come?
Customer: November. The first Saturday would be ideal. We discussed October, but our tutor will be away then. December is too close to the winter market, when everybody is busy.
Receptionist: The first Saturday in November is free. How many people are coming? We need the total, including your tutor, for the room layout.
Customer: Eighteen altogether. There are sixteen learners, the tutor and me. Two other people were interested but cannot get childcare, so they will join a later workshop.
Receptionist: In that case I recommend the Willow room. It holds twenty-four comfortably and has large windows. The Cedar room is bigger, but it faces the car park and can be noisy when deliveries arrive.
Customer: Willow sounds right. Natural light will be useful when people photograph objects indoors. Can the tables be moved into small groups, or are they fixed?
Receptionist: They are movable. Our caretaker can arrange three groups before you arrive. You can use the whiteboard at no extra charge. What other equipment would you like?
Customer: Please reserve a projector. The tutor will bring her own laptop and camera, and I will bring an extension cable. We do not need any printed handouts from the centre.
Receptionist: That is fine. There is a kitchen beside the room. Tea and coffee are included, but we do not normally provide lunch at weekends.
Customer: We will bring sandwiches. One learner has a serious allergy to nuts, though. Is there somewhere we can store food separately?
Receptionist: Yes, there is a small refrigerator in the kitchen. I will leave a labelled shelf clear. Please remind the other participants about the allergy before they prepare anything to share. We cannot guarantee that the building has never contained nuts.
Customer: Understood. What will the room cost for the day?
Receptionist: The usual rate is sixty pounds, but registered community groups pay forty-five pounds. That includes the projector. Heating and cleaning are included too, provided you leave the tables clear.
Customer: We are registered, so forty-five is good. The workshop starts at nine. Could I get in earlier to meet the tutor and set out a few sample photographs?
Receptionist: The caretaker opens the building at eight thirty. I suggest you arrive at eight forty; that gives him time to finish his checks. Participants should wait outside the room until five to nine.
Customer: I will put eight forty in my calendar. Is there a deposit?
Receptionist: Ten pounds, which comes off the final bill. Please pay it by Monday. Friday is our normal deadline for weekday courses, but weekend bookings are handled separately. I will email the payment link today.
Customer: Thank you. Please include the cancellation policy as well.
Receptionist: Certainly. You can cancel without further charge up to seven days beforehand. We encourage you to use public transport; the number six bus stops opposite the entrance. I will send directions with your confirmation.
Customer: That is everything. Thank you for being so helpful.
TEXT,
