    'il-tour' => [
        'kind' => 'audio',
        'title' => 'Part 2 · A welcome tour of Riverside Park',
        'audio_script' => <<<'TEXT'
Good morning, everyone, and welcome to Riverside Park. My name is Helen and I am one of the volunteer guides. Before you explore, I will explain how the park developed, then describe some of the buildings you can visit. There are printed maps on the table, but you do not need one to follow this introduction.

Although this may look like an old country estate, it was never a private garden. For almost a century this land was occupied by a factory making railway equipment. When production moved elsewhere, the council bought the site. Local residents wanted an open place to walk, so the abandoned industrial land became a public park. Some foundations remain beneath the meadow. We have left one short section of railway track visible to show where finished equipment was loaded.

Entry to the grounds is free every day. Our guided walking tours are popular, however, and places must be reserved in advance. You can make a reservation online or speak to staff here. Buying a ticket at the gate will not secure a place on a tour. Children under twelve can join without paying, but they still need a reservation because group sizes are limited.

You will notice a large metal sculpture near the pond. The artist deliberately left the surface unpainted. As rain and air affect the metal, its colour gradually changes. That changing appearance is part of the work, rather than a sign that we have neglected it. Please look closely, but do not climb on the sculpture. We have other installations that visitors are invited to touch, and those are marked with a hand symbol.

This evening we plan to hold a lantern walk. Light rain will not stop it: most of the route is sheltered, and the lanterns are waterproof. Strong wind is the one condition that will make us cancel, because branches could fall along the woodland path. We will post a decision on the park website by four o'clock. If it is cancelled, ticket holders can choose a refund or a place next month.

Now let me tell you where to find our services. The old stable, beside the central lawn, has been converted into a cafe. It serves soup and sandwiches as well as drinks. The courtyard outside is the designated picnic area, so you may eat your own food there. Please leave the indoor cafe tables for customers during busy periods. Tap water is available free of charge.

The glasshouse is used for gardening classes. A class may be working inside when you visit, but you are welcome to watch from the marked walkway. We no longer keep the tropical plant collection there because heating the building through winter was too expensive. The instructors now demonstrate methods that visitors can use in ordinary gardens, including growing vegetables in small containers.

In the water tower there is a display of historical photographs. A lift takes visitors to the exhibition floor, though the narrow staircase above it remains closed. The pictures were lent by families whose relatives worked in the factory, and their recorded memories can be heard through headphones. Allow about twenty minutes if you want to see the whole display.

The riverside lodge provides a quiet study room. It has desks and sockets, but no phone calls are permitted inside. The building at the east gate is our bicycle repair point. Volunteers can help with a loose chain or a puncture, although they cannot carry out major repairs. Finally, lost property is kept in the visitor centre, where we are standing now. If you find a bag or a coat, please bring it here instead of leaving it at the nearest building. Staff can record where it was found and contact its owner.

Enjoy your visit, keep to the paths around the restoration meadow, and ask any member of staff if you need assistance.
TEXT,
