Maya: So that topic is out. We also discussed food waste, but another group is studying the dining halls. I think our strongest idea is to investigate why students repair things instead of replacing them. There is a repair workshop on campus, yet some people have never heard of it.
Leo: Yes, let us focus on decisions about repair. We can include clothes and small household objects, but leave bicycles out because the transport society already collects detailed information about them. That should keep the research manageable.
Maya: Dr Shah liked the idea, but she warned us about relying only on what people say they would do. Students may say that they care about waste and still throw something away when repairing it takes time.
Leo: Right. She wants us to compare stated intentions with actual behaviour. We could ask about the last broken object someone owned, rather than asking what an environmentally responsible person ought to do. We should make clear that we are not judging their choices.
Maya: Who should we interview? It would be easy to recruit everyone from our own course, but then they would all know the same facilities.
Leo: Let us include students in their first year and their final year. That would let us compare newcomers with people who have had longer to discover the workshop. We do not need equal numbers from every department for this small exploratory study.
Maya: Before the interviews, we need a short questionnaire to find willing participants. You offered to draft it. Is that still all right?
Leo: Yes. I tried an early version on twelve students yesterday. That pilot was useful: several thought repair meant only paying a professional. They did not include sewing on a button themselves. We need to define repair at the start, using examples without suggesting a preferred answer.
Maya: Twelve is enough to find that kind of problem. Did they understand the questions about cost?
Leo: Mostly, though one person wanted an option for an item they had received as a gift. I have added that. The form should take about four minutes now, rather than the eight minutes we estimated.
Maya: Where should we distribute it? The student union gets busy, but many people rush through on their way to events.
Leo: I have permission to stand in the library entrance. People often have a few spare minutes there while waiting for friends. We can use the tablets provided by the department, so nobody has to type a long website address into a phone.
Maya: Good. We can offer a voucher as the prize in a draw. The campus bookshop has agreed to provide one. It will not be cash, and participation must be voluntary whether or not someone enters the draw.
Leo: I will add a separate contact form so the survey answers do not contain names. What is our next deadline?
