        'paragraphs' => [
            'A. On a hot afternoon, a tree-lined street can feel like a different city from an exposed square a few blocks away. Buildings and asphalt absorb solar energy and release it slowly, allowing urban temperatures to remain high after sunset. Trees interrupt this process in two main ways: their crowns cast shade, and water evaporating from their leaves carries heat into the air. The second process, called transpiration, is less visible than shade but can be equally important. For a city trying to protect residents during heatwaves, counting newly planted trees is therefore an appealing measure of progress. Yet the count alone says little about where cooling occurs or who can benefit from it.',
