        'title' => 'Reading passage 2 · The clock inside a seed',
        'paragraphs' => [
            'A. A seed that does not germinate may be perfectly alive. Although warmth and moisture are often described as the requirements for growth, many species also have a mechanism that prevents germination at an unsuitable moment. This condition is called dormancy. It should not be confused with death, or with the simple absence of water. A dry seed may begin growing as soon as it becomes wet, while a dormant seed can remain inactive in apparently favourable conditions. This delay is valuable: the first mild day after a cold spell does not necessarily mark the end of winter. Germinating too early can cost a plant its only opportunity to reproduce.',
            'B. Long-term conservation adds another complication. Many seeds tolerate careful drying and can be stored at low temperatures in seed banks. Such seeds are often described as orthodox. Their internal activity becomes extremely slow, although periodic tests are still needed to check their condition. Other species produce seeds that are damaged by substantial drying. These are known as recalcitrant seeds, and ordinary dry storage cannot preserve them safely. Conservators may have to maintain living collections or investigate other techniques. A freezer full of packets therefore cannot represent every tree or wildflower. Decisions about a collection must begin with the biology of the species, rather than an assumption that all seeds behave alike.',
            'C. For some temperate plants, a prolonged period of moist cold provides a useful signal. Gardeners imitate it by placing seeds in damp material at a controlled low temperature, a treatment known as stratification. The duration needed varies among species, and cold alone is not always enough. Subsequent warmth, light or a particular alternation of day and night temperatures may also be required. These combined signals reduce the chance of a response to a brief accident of weather. A warmer winter can consequently delay or prevent germination in species that fail to receive sufficient chilling. A higher average temperature does not automatically mean that every stage of plant development will happen earlier.',
