        'title' => 'Reading passage 3 · When experts think together',
        'paragraphs' => [
            'A. Inviting several people to solve a problem seems an obvious way to improve a decision. Each person may know something the others do not, and errors might cancel out when estimates are combined. In suitable circumstances this expectation is justified. If people independently estimate a quantity, such as the number of objects in a container, the median can be closer to the truth than many individual guesses. But the word independently is doing essential work. If everyone copies the first confident answer, collecting twenty estimates may add little information. A group can contain considerable expertise while still failing to use it. The central question is therefore not simply how many experts to invite, but how their knowledge enters the discussion.',
            'B. A common difficulty concerns information known to everyone. Imagine a committee choosing between two research projects. All members have read the same short summary, but each has also visited a different laboratory. During discussion, the shared summary tends to dominate: mentioning it feels relevant because other members recognise it and can respond immediately. A detail from one laboratory may receive less attention precisely because it is unfamiliar. Yet those unique details could reverse the committee’s preference when combined. Merely instructing members to be thorough may not solve the problem. Giving each person a protected opportunity to report information that others lack makes the missing evidence more likely to enter the record before a choice is made.',
            'C. Social pressure produces a second difficulty. A junior researcher may notice an error but hesitate after a senior colleague has endorsed the result. Silence then appears to confirm agreement, encouraging everyone else to remain quiet. Open disagreement is useful only when people can express it without expecting humiliation or punishment. Some groups assign a rotating critic whose task is to test the emerging argument. Rotation matters because a permanent critic can be dismissed as predictably negative. Anonymous first-round comments can also help, although they remove some opportunities to ask follow-up questions. No single procedure guarantees honesty; the chair must show through ordinary behaviour that a well-supported objection is a contribution rather than a personal attack.',
